#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 960.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097469} :activity/id #uuid "f64fb60f-19cd-5d89-bcbf-86fc646151c5" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048882 :topic/id #uuid "c968c8f6-9b7b-5304-a2de-0e2f2c824aa9" :topic/math-academy-id 1101} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097469 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097829 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have a vector $\\overrightarrow{AB}=\\textbf{a}.$ We denote its **magnitude**, also known as its *modulus* or *length*, as $|\\overrightarrow{AB}|$ or $|\\textbf{a}|.$\n\nFor example, the vector below has a magnitude of $8,$ denoted $|\\overrightarrow{AB}|=8.$\n\n![](images/71/71105c9d5920ec71e61fe1176021024188a86bd8023671b79e0ce89082994299.png)\n\nNow, let's consider the following situation. Suppose that the vector $\\textbf{a}$ is directed vertically upwards and $|\\textbf{a}|=6,$ while $\\textbf{b}$ is directed horizontally to the right and $|\\textbf{b}|=8.$ How can we find $|\\textbf{a}+\\textbf{b}|?$\n\nFirst, we use the triangle law of addition to find the resultant $\\textbf{a}+\\textbf{b}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/ff/ffd155d3b72015a72d878fc8089381a5bcf5a97f698f85945642189ea7368a3f.png)\n\nNotice that we obtain a right triangle. So, we can use the Pythagorean theorem as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\text{a}+\\text{b}{|}^{2} & =|\\text{a}{|}^{2}+|\\text{b}{|}^{2} \\\\  & ={6}^{2}+{8}^{2} \\\\  & =100\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n|\\textbf{a}+\\textbf{b}|=\\sqrt{100}=10.\n$$\n\n**Important**: In the above calculation we wrote $|\\mathbf{a}+\\mathbf{b}|^2 = |\\mathbf{a}|^2+|\\mathbf{b}|^2$. This works only when the vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ are at right angles, and the relationship is not true in general. We'll see how to deal with non-right-angled vectors soon." :tutorial/id #uuid "2584bcd4-11ba-581a-a0b2-10a7b2854dfc" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2902 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "15159922-befb-577b-93fb-cc8cd2981961" :step/math-academy-id 15687 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097471}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097471 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096643 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096440 :question/id #uuid "8cc1617d-ba3f-5ae3-aca0-90354ffbd62f" :question/math-academy-id "e-4273"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "48579a8d-61f0-50c1-81f6-7b087b3f936c" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049224} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049603}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096955} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097008} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186571392} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186571426} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186571436} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186571437} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572112} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572122}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Magnitude of a Sum of Vectors Using the Pythagorean Theorem"} :step/id #uuid "ec39e834-5cb5-545e-b5d3-96ee21673966" :step/math-academy-id 15689 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097547}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097474 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096645 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096442 :question/id #uuid "f0c94cce-790d-50b0-bd9b-13b64867848b" :question/math-academy-id "e-4275"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c145c97c-c313-597f-b84e-8adb3bdf9c14" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050710}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096956} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097017} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572128} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572176}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Magnitude of a Sum of Vectors Using the Law of Cosines"} :step/id #uuid "f595e55d-b2aa-50bf-8d57-1c6eaf004504" :step/math-academy-id 15692} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097491 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097839 :tutorial/content "When the triangle law for addition does *not* give a right triangle, we *cannot* use the Pythagorean theorem to compute the magnitude of the sum.\n\nFor example, using the triangle law to draw the sum $\\mathbf{a}+\\mathbf{b}$ for the vectors below, we do not get a right triangle. Instead, we get a triangle with an obtuse angle $\\theta.$\n\n![](images/96/967966ec9d8ac0b7358623645de0e7931ef4d6cc87bd919d1f0cf04f7ca6ca28.png)\n\nWe do not obtain a right triangle here, so we cannot use the Pythagorean theorem. However, we can use the law of cosines:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{|a+b|}^{2} & ={|a|}^{2}+{|b|}^{2}-2|a||b|\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn general, given any two vectors that form a triangle using the triangle addition law, we can always use the law of cosines to compute the magnitude of the sum." :tutorial/id #uuid "1a74f53c-fedc-56dc-80cb-691a8a057007" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2962 :tutorial/title "Calculating the Magnitude of a Vector Using the Law of Cosines"} :step/id #uuid "ff54536c-3852-5ce2-8d8f-43b7291868cb" :step/math-academy-id 16029 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097748}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097547 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096690 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096485 :question/id #uuid "ba160db3-7799-5a25-8c50-5147100f7475" :question/math-academy-id "e-5013"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4e0146b8-421b-5d63-a933-3de9bcaa7ab2" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097159} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097172} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572123} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572148} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186575525} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186575531} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186575556}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Magnitude of a Vector From a Diagram"} :step/id #uuid "2412a5fc-6f07-508c-bfdf-341b7a606b74" :step/math-academy-id 18402 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097491}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097748 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097805 :tutorial/content "![](images/0a/0ac79071675ca17dbb25708f8131a0f1706c29ffe69c750969112fd6a097d1f6.png)\n\nConsider the diagram above, where $|\\mathbf{a}|=4$ and $|\\mathbf{b}|=5.$ How can we find $|\\mathbf{a+b}|?$ \n\nFirst, we use the triangle law of addition to find $\\mathbf{a}+\\mathbf{b}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/dd/dd9b407a04394d9648db4671788791a8cf615b7755c1efead6f637fe26a40562.png)\n\nWe do not obtain a right triangle here, so we cannot use the Pythagorean theorem. But we can use the law of cosines, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{|a+b|}^{2} & ={|a|}^{2}+{|b|}^{2}-2|a||b|\\operatorname{cos}⁡({120}^{∘}) \\\\  & ={4}^{2}+{5}^{2}-2\\cdot 4\\cdot 5\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =16+25+20 \\\\  & =61\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $|\\mathbf{a+b}|=\\sqrt{61}.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "9bdfe6fa-0c60-5f19-a5e8-3d7a07872026" :tutorial/math-academy-id 13222 :tutorial/title "A Worked Example"} :step/id #uuid "1e20290a-9c6e-5b7f-9e29-a74378de3216" :step/math-academy-id 457894 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097474}}] :activity/title "The Magnitude of a Vector" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186096881}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046647} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186575668} :activity/id #uuid "cb643399-c9f1-5f69-aaa9-f0f416dc44e9" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048882 :topic/id #uuid "c968c8f6-9b7b-5304-a2de-0e2f2c824aa9" :topic/math-academy-id 1101} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186575637 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186575525 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b371911-bbad-59a4-a192-de4fbe2c032e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10\\sqrt{13}\\,\\text{mm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186574992} {:answer/id #uuid "4d190923-3c5e-5440-913a-9e45d38e8136" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6\\sqrt{19}\\,\\text{mm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186575081} {:answer/id #uuid "6e86868b-8f86-560f-ad61-ab7f7b2f1b12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{mm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186575149} {:answer/id #uuid "cd1104ea-933b-5ad2-b216-bcff58d29534" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "50\\,\\text{mm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186575313} {:answer/id #uuid "d30b7b97-b363-5da7-90da-205bd7013318" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5\\sqrt{17}\\,\\text{mm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186575322}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186574992} :answer-field/id #uuid "17441dbe-6903-5e53-bc8a-41e64fe4b46d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186575417}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "009a4381-6aca-5a6f-b010-2dbc7da60371" :question/math-academy-id "q-118921" :question/problem "![](images/7b/7b676396ae24fe47c832e4442b67a9ba66128eff11a8c08f7570704c0a16eeb7.png)\n\nConsider the picture shown above. Find $|a+b|.$" :question/worked-solution "We use the triangle law of addition for $a$ and $b.$ We can start $a$ from an arbitrary point on the plane. The resultant goes from the start of $a$ to the head of $b.$\n\n![](images/87/87a40729c97eddcba2820dc083002aad9ee4d05d7e9e654bcf51d8410a2e4861.png)\n\nNow, in $△AHB$ we have $∠AHB={90}^{∘}$, $AH=30\\,\\text{mm}$ and $BH=20\\,\\text{mm}.$ So, the Pythagorean theorem gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|a+b| & =AB \\\\  & =\\sqrt{A{H}^{2}+B{H}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{30}^{2}+{20}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1\\,300} \\\\  & =10\\sqrt{13}\\,\\text{mm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "1de77141-d944-56f2-a5e7-2f43def28347"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186575668 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186575531 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1206f0b9-be3a-5159-98e0-da77b6c0445a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5\\sqrt{41}\\,\\text{mm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186574979} {:answer/id #uuid "2ecb5427-5ac5-59b1-94a2-f82f393f874e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{13}\\,\\text{mm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186575026} {:answer/id #uuid "432d23e6-e40e-5e8a-a566-7d880e136d59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "20\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{mm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186575067} {:answer/id #uuid "5d0fa895-1079-53e7-945b-0a3924af1dff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "40\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{mm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186575114} {:answer/id #uuid "e3afd05d-c063-5ddb-abee-56e9a09c27ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "25\\,\\text{mm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186575352}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186574979} :answer-field/id #uuid "c48d75c9-8417-5647-a9f6-2a5523919321" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186575494}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "1a2c5f5b-bc36-5bd5-afb2-0b0c9dcdd330" :question/math-academy-id "q-20202" :question/problem "![](images/86/868b0274063a19084dac216568a9ed3cebedcc82d967ed6d89ce62f96d38c336.png)\n\nConsider the picture shown above. Find $|a|.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/a0/a051a9158e8147415b605fee37aef3ef4862281da1c2a0387fd40aa99bddee49.png)\n\nIn $△AHB$ we have $∠AHB={90}^{∘}$, $AH=20\\,\\text{mm}$ and $BH=25\\,\\text{mm}.$ So, the Pythagorean theorem gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =\\sqrt{A{H}^{2}+B{H}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{20}^{2}+{25}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1\\,025} \\\\  & =5\\sqrt{41}\\,\\text{mm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "8c5c1ce5-8d99-5032-b6c2-6072a99ca917" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186575681}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186575681 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186575556 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04492ea7-d25b-56ea-ae0e-47957b737f4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{mm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186574959} {:answer/id #uuid "6bcce013-cdbc-58c4-a357-a196757423be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5\\sqrt{10}\\,\\text{mm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186575143} {:answer/id #uuid "b095cbfc-1472-52b1-804b-05c032bb0e53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "25\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{mm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186575264} {:answer/id #uuid "ba28057b-647d-5124-b1a6-35e530999312" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{25}\\,\\text{mm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186575280} {:answer/id #uuid "bcd0818b-9462-55a1-9ed5-46fc015e79ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "25\\,\\text{mm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186575288}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186575264} :answer-field/id #uuid "87ff2314-8fb7-54d5-87c5-d6b7e1e45b40" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186575469}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "6aaad334-a3c0-5643-a528-243af5b47439" :question/math-academy-id "q-118915" :question/problem "![](images/de/de4864b00e5b51431ef019a930c3980e33c0f47812c9ee09743934128a72644b.png)\n\nConsider the picture shown above. Find $|a|.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/ab/ab521b7fc8b594fa7a8a97cf1b2dc57884d50d7941f0e6f7f6d56d3179d3c7a3.png)\n\nIn $△AHB$ we have $∠AHB={90}^{∘}$, $AH=25\\,\\text{mm}$ and $BH=25\\,\\text{mm}.$ So, the Pythagorean theorem gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =\\sqrt{A{H}^{2}+B{H}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{25}^{2}+{25}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2\\cdot {25}^{2}} \\\\  & =25\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{mm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "bd40e0b0-1d40-522a-981c-caef9e15c387" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186575637}}] :activity/title "The Magnitude of a Vector" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186574948}]}
